-- title:
--   Counterexample structure (m) — A minimum counterexample has no abelian congruence other than equality
-- statement:
--   Let $(M,\diamond)$ be a finite E677 magma that fails E255 at a point $z$, that is, $q(z)\ne z$ with $q(z)=((z\diamond z)\diamond z)\diamond z$. Suppose that $M$ has minimum order: every E677 operation on $\{0,\dots,n-1\}$ with $n<|M|$ satisfies E255.
--
--   A congruence $\theta$ of $M$ is abelian when it satisfies the term condition $C(\theta,\theta;0)$: for every term function $p$ of arity $k+1$ (a map $M^{k+1}\to M$ given by a $\diamond$-term), all $x\,\theta\,y$ and all $c_1\,\theta\,d_1,\dots,c_k\,\theta\,d_k$,
--
--   $$p(x,c_1,\dots,c_k)=p(x,d_1,\dots,d_k)\ \text{ gives }\ p(y,c_1,\dots,c_k)=p(y,d_1,\dots,d_k).$$
--
--   **Theorem.** Every abelian congruence of $M$ is the equality relation.
--
--   **Use in a counterexample search.** In the normal form of Counterexample structure (k), the congruence generated by $(z,q(z))$ is the least congruence other than equality (the monolith). By this theorem the monolith is not abelian, and $M$ is not an abelian algebra (take $\theta$ universal). For example, $M$ is not an affine extension $(x,s)\diamond(y,t)=(x\diamond y,\ \alpha_{x,y}s+\beta_{x,y}t+c_{x,y})$ over an abelian group with at least two elements, with endomorphisms $\alpha_{x,y},\beta_{x,y}$: there "same first coordinate" is an abelian congruence (by induction on terms; not part of the Lean theorem). ETP blueprint Lemma 13.4 already excludes these extensions; the theorem needs no such coordinates.
--
--   **Proof idea.** Only the term $x_1\diamond x_0$ is used. For this term, the term condition says: two $\theta$-related rows that agree in a column $t$ agree in every column $\theta$-related to $t$. If $\theta$ relates two distinct elements, then $M/\theta$ is a smaller E677 magma, so it satisfies E255, and $q(z)\,\theta\,z$. Counterexample structure (g) then gives two $\theta$-related rows that agree in column $z$ and differ in a column $\theta$-related to $z$.
--
--   **Prior work.** The term condition is standard (R. Freese and R. McKenzie, *Commutator Theory for Congruence Modular Varieties*, Definitions 3.2 and 5.3; with one distinguished variable as in M. Bonatto and D. Stanovský, arXiv:1902.08980v3). The step from row agreement to E255 is T. Tao's argument (ETP Zulip thread "FINITE: 677 -> 255", 2024-12-14). B. Le Floch (same thread, 2024-12-14) proved that the affine extensions above satisfy E255 when the base does (ETP blueprint Lemma 13.4). S. Chuks-Onah (same thread, June 2026) proposed to exclude a minimal counterexample through its monolith $\mu$ by Lemma 13.4 when the step $M\to M/\mu$ is affine; one step of his argument that it is affine was not proved. No earlier statement for every abelian congruence was found in the ETP blueprint, the ETP paper (arXiv:2512.07087v2), the ETP Zulip thread up to 2026-10-14, Freese–McKenzie, Burris–Sankappanavar, or papers on left quasigroups and racks.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The hypotheses `h`, `hmin`, `z`, `hz`, `r`, `hr` and `hcompat` are those of Counterexample structure (b), so the theorem applies directly to the output of (k). A term function of arity $k+1$ is a function `p : (Fin (k + 1) → α) → α` that satisfies every predicate `T` that holds at the projections `fun v => v i` and is closed under pointwise `op`. The tuple $(x,c_1,\dots,c_k)$ is `Fin.cons x c`, and the term condition is `habel`.
